eah will check what is the correct modelling for it dataclass or Interface", "createdAt": "2026-11-04T12:20:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/cram2/cognitive_robot_abstract_machine/pull/866#discussion_r4467355627"}]}}, {"id": "PRRT_kwDOQga1Oc6zkACV", "isResolved": false, "isOutdated": true, "path": "krrood/src/krrood/planning/state.py", "line": null, "comments": {"nodes": [{"databaseId": 4466749927, "author": {"login": "tomsch420"}, "body": "now this sounds like mixing terminology. why is the class GroundTerm not called fluent or vice versa?", "createdAt": "2026-11-04T11:13:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/cram2/cognitive_robot_abstract_machine/pull/866#discussion_r4466749927"}, {"databaseId": 4467371893, "author": {"login": "AbdelrhmanBassiouny"}, "body": "hmm, yeah, I think currently a Fluent is a GroundTerm, but also the GroundTerm is not fully grounded in the sense that it's value is not set, what is ground about it is that it's not over many variables, it's a literal like with one value in it's domain, and a mapping where the value is not set, so an instance with an attribute name for example, the instance is already one thing not a set of instances like a normal variable, but it's attribute value is changing it is not set yet for the Term, that is what the TermReader actually does, it reads values for terms. But maybe we can find a more correct name for GroundTerm, maybe Fluent? FluentTerm? or something else will check with claude and what the literature say about that.", "createdAt": "2026-11-04T12:22:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/cram2/cognitive_robot_abstract_machine/pull/866#discussion_r4467371893"}]}}, {"id": "PRRT_kwDOQga1Oc6z3mh8", "isResolved": false, "isOutdated": false, "path": "krrood/src/krrood/class_diagrams/call_signature.py", "line": 37, "comments": {"nodes": [{"databaseId": 4475128613, "author": {"login": "LucaKro"}, "body": "nice, i really dislike claude naming like `of` etc", "createdAt": "2026-11-05T00:32:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/c